nd a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-2I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-2I)^{2}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-2I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A-2I)v=0$ is ${v}_{2}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-2I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-2I)v=0.$ \n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175986 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e72b8c-ae67-5344-93bc-3a6f89255514" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Generalized Eigenvector of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95655b2b-d339-5c65-b26a-3316c2afaa3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :a