 #uuid "02c9a095-fd9d-56ba-ba47-e3e219dbe6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368747} {:answer/id #uuid "f7277e49-6288-5584-a09c-ddda66e39db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368748} {:answer/id #uuid "db384674-af4f-5ff2-8bb1-1bccbffc0a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368749} {:answer/id #uuid "97c7f316-647e-519f-902d-90eb0492f4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02c9a095-fd9d-56ba-ba47-e3e219dbe6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368747} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e2a0d1a-8aaf-5755-a625-95b675a14933" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6776a47d-75c0-5904-90e8-1e670448c05c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78503" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-3I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,(A-3I)^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda =3,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-3I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-3I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-3I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-3I)^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-3I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-3I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-3I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A-3I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A-3I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{3}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-3I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A-3I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-3I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$"}]}
